s wife, desisted, pouring down the water at his own feet. Hence the name of Kalmāşapāda.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.464,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-70-32","DUTT-1-70-33"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1069027","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b4d805707838f7f42a2c25fc66b91d83af47b240ec4341b3acbfd6b5563b8621","compact":"c6697a01fdde00374a7c805e47be0034cab67fc783a5ebaaabd38b859d841f1b","nasal":"175837b2e630339bb5c7aa35bff7aa0bf6045013a20c9f5e7eae521778682fc3"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-69-28","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":69,"verse":28,"english":"And he came finally to be known by the name of Kalmāşapāda.* And from him sprung Śarkhana. And Sudarśana was Sankhana's and Agnivarna was Sudaśana's son. He incurred Vasiştha's curse, and was turned into a Rākşasa. He took up water, intending to clear scores with Vasistha; but at the request of his wife, desisted, pouring down the water at his own feet. Hence the name of Kalmāşapāda. Śīghraga was Agnivarņa’s, and Maru was Śīghraga's son. And Maru's son was Prasuśruka, and from Praśuśruka sprung Ambarīşa.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.471,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-70-33","DUTT-1-70-34"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1069028","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"66c70e556c0d69635726a501e5a736a3e162e8c93cde7fa0d3c00f8ac9354ad5","compact":"36b72a35bbb20a8430d4bb2512d142b074cbbbc091e92ba0f9f0794e6e35b6d5","nasal":"83dee77b3d50363cf8afaa464ecfb2ba7e991750b6297a4a6305b2133eec41f2"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-69-29","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":69,"verse":29,"english":"Śīghraga was Agnivarņa’s, and Maru was Śīghraga's son. And Maru's son was Prasuśruka, and from Praśuśruka sprung Ambarīşa.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.507,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-70-34"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1069029","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"d2fcf335fe15d64eac1da6f76fa73ad156f0da13b9b7ebe092921988daa9501d","compact":"c0137080e786f2ea7265b5533c4c2515068f74b33f973c50e78c66b491b1ba13","nasal":"dba4db654a800136f7d2fd5e713e520fdf8b471b7630680a8723da595dc89dc7"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-69-30","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":69,"verse":30,"english":"Ambarisa's son was Nahusa, lord of earth. And Nahusa's son was Yayāti, was Nābhāga. And Nābhāga's son was Aja, and from Aja sprung Dasaratha. And from this Dasaratha have come the brothers Rāma and Lakşmaņa.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.91,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-70-35"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1069030","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"498dea5de01b9ace328c6e25b1a43432552b1861c0bfb38440ab503ec23a554c","compact":"10e6d0c32e43770bdd5df6067e7d3d2db54f07a988bec9bce1fd488b32081a15","nasal":"10e6d0c32e43770bdd5df6067e7d3d2db54f07a988bec9bce1fd488b32081a15"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-69-31","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":69,"verse":31,"english":"It is in the interests of Rāma and Lakşmaņa belonging to the heroic and truthful and pious sovereigns sprung in the Ikşvāku line, and possessing purity of race even from the time of their founder, that, Oking, we solicit the hands of your daughters. And, O foremost of men, it be-hove you to confer like brides upon like bridegrooms.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-70-36"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1069031","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a774a317d216c2526939a55cc6d253dc78842c8c69d1aa73100014f82831e6fe","compact":"2ec842e02d2b0cea52a6794f651dbbfa0a07bdf7875442827220ff8a4b26a7b1","nasal":"2